IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `lsl-dates`

-- Long Service Leave Act 1976 (ACT) -- the questions of the employee (and, after the employee's death, the employee's
-- legal personal representative).
--
-- SEAT: the employee: a person employed in the ACT private sector who claims long service leave, payment for it, payment
-- instead of it when employment ends, or pro rata payment; after the employee's death, the legal personal representative
-- who claims the payment (ss 11A (2) (d), 11C (1) (d)). "Am I an employee for this Act?", "Does this break count against
-- my service?", "When do I become entitled?", "How much leave do I have?", "Must my employer let me take it, and tell me
-- 60 days ahead?", "At what rate must I be paid?", "What am I owed when my job ends, and by when?". The employer is the
-- text's actor; what it did is asked as a fact. The registrar and the authorised officer get no seat.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a number, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an
-- EVENT. Three duties with deadlines are regulative rules run inside questions: the employer's written notice of the
-- day leave starts (s 6 (2)), its payment for the leave credit within 90 days after employment ends (s 11A (4)), and its
-- compliance with an authorised officer's notice within 28 days (s 13E).
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: the whole Act except the machinery. Part 1 ss 1-2B are formal; part 2 ss 2C-2G, 3, 4, 6-11,
-- 11A, 11C, 11D, 12 (2); part 3 s 13D (4), s 13E (and s 13F, 13G only in a gloss); part 4 s 14; the dictionary
-- definitions of employee, continuous service, ordinary remuneration, salary or wages. Out: s 12 (1) and (3) record
-- contents and inspection, ss 12A-13CE and 13J-13K (officers, entry, identity cards, privilege), s 18, and the endnotes.
--
-- Edition: Long Service Leave Act 1976 (ACT), A1976-27, Republication No 29 (R29), effective 19 November 2025,
-- deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/A1976-27.txt.
--
-- (c) Australian Capital Territory. Used under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md). Unofficial text.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to someone else are INPUTS. Answers follow
-- the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): the text's own no is marked "(the text's no)"; an
-- answer where the text is limited by its own wording, or silent, is marked "NOTHING-kind".

§ `Long Service Leave Act 1976: the questions of the employee`

§§ `Dictionary, s 2C -- Am I an employee for this Act`

-- Dictionary: employee (a) includes a person remunerated at piecework rates, a part-time employee and a casual employee;
-- (b) does not include a public employee or a person employed by an MLA under the Legislative Assembly (Members' Staff)
-- Act 1989, s 10. s 2C: a person may be an employee even though paid completely or partly by commission. "Employee" is
-- otherwise not defined: whether you are employed at all (rather than self-employed) is a borrowed term, asked as a fact.
DECLARE `Am I an employee for this Act` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you are an employee for this Act (dictionary, employee; s 2C for commission)`
    `no: a public employee is not an employee for this Act (the text's no, dictionary, employee (b) (i))`
    `no: a person employed by a member of the Legislative Assembly under the Legislative Assembly (Members' Staff) Act 1989, s 10, is not an employee for this Act (the text's no, dictionary, employee (b) (ii))`
    `the Act does not define who is employed; on these facts you are not employed by anyone, and the Act says nothing more (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Am I an employee covered by the ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976 (dictionary, employee; s 2C)? Part-time and casual workers, pieceworkers and people paid by commission are included; public employees and staff employed by a member of the Legislative Assembly are not.
GIVEN `employed by someone` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you work for an employer as an employee (full-time, part-time, casual, on piecework rates or paid by commission), rather than as a self-employed contractor
      `a public employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a public employee of the ACT (for example an ACT public servant)
      `employed by a member of the Legislative Assembly` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are employed by a member of the ACT Legislative Assembly under the Legislative Assembly (Members' Staff) Act 1989, section 10
GIVETH AN `Am I an employee for this Act`
`dictionary -- am I an employee for this Act` `employed by someone` `a public employee` `employed by a member of the Legislative Assembly` MEANS
    IF `a public employee`
    THEN `no: a public employee is not an employee for this Act (the text's no, dictionary, employee (b) (i))`
    ELSE IF `employed by a member of the Legislative Assembly`
         THEN `no: a person employed by a member of the Legislative Assembly under the Legislative Assembly (Members' Staff) Act 1989, s 10, is not an employee for this Act (the text's no, dictionary, employee (b) (ii))`
         ELSE IF `employed by someone`
              THEN `yes: you are an employee for this Act (dictionary, employee; s 2C for commission)`
              ELSE `the Act does not define who is employed; on these facts you are not employed by anyone, and the Act says nothing more (NOTHING-kind)`

§§ `Dictionary, s 2F -- My ordinary remuneration`

-- Dictionary: ordinary remuneration means the sum of (a) the salary or wages payable; (b) allowances for skill,
-- qualifications, board and lodging; (c) amounts under a bonus, performance pay or incentive scheme usually paid with
-- the salary or wages; (d) where the employer provides board and lodging, its value. Salary or wages does not include
-- overtime, penalty rates, or allowances an award or agreement excludes from the rate for overtime.
@export What is my ordinary remuneration for a week, for the ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976 (dictionary, ordinary remuneration)? It is your salary or wages (without overtime or penalty rates), plus allowances for skill, qualifications, board and lodging, usual bonuses and incentive payments, and the value of any board and lodging your employer provides.
GIVEN `my weekly salary or wages` IS A NUMBER @desc Your salary or wages for a week, in dollars, leaving out overtime, penalty rates, and allowances that your award or agreement leaves out of the overtime rate
      `my weekly skill and qualification allowances` IS A NUMBER @desc Allowances paid to you each week for skill or qualifications, in dollars
      `my weekly board and lodging allowances` IS A NUMBER @desc Allowances paid to you each week for board and lodging, in dollars
      `my usual weekly bonus or incentive pay` IS A NUMBER @desc Amounts paid to you each week under a bonus, performance pay or incentive scheme that are usually paid with your salary or wages, in dollars (0 if none)
      `the weekly value of board and lodging my employer provides` IS A NUMBER @desc If your employer gives you board and lodging, its value for a week, in dollars (0 if not)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`dictionary -- my ordinary remuneration for a week` `my weekly salary or wages` `my weekly skill and qualification allowances` `my weekly board and lodging allowances` `my usual weekly bonus or incentive pay` `the weekly value of board and lodging my employer provides` MEANS
    `my weekly salary or wages`
        PLUS `my weekly skill and qualification allowances`
        PLUS `my weekly board and lodging allowances`
        PLUS `my usual weekly bonus or incentive pay`
        PLUS `the weekly value of board and lodging my employer provides`

-- s 2F: an employee paid completely by commission, or partly by salary or wages and partly by commission, during a
-- year, is taken to be paid completely by salary or wages, and the amount for a week is the total payable for the year
-- (commission, salary or wages) divided by 52.
@export If I am paid wholly or partly by commission, what are my weekly salary or wages for working out my long service leave pay (ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976, s 2F)? They are the total paid to you for the year as commission, salary or wages, divided by 52.
GIVEN `the total payable for the year` IS A NUMBER @desc The total amount payable to you for the year as commission, salary or wages, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 2F -- my weekly salary or wages when paid by commission` `the total payable for the year` MEANS
    `the total payable for the year` DIVIDED BY 52

§§ `s 2G and the dictionary -- Breaks in my service`

-- s 2G (1): period of service means a period of continuous service as the employee of a particular employer.
-- s 2G (2): these interruptions do not break continuity: (a) an industrial dispute, if the employee returns in accordance
-- with the terms of settlement; (b) a stand-down for slackness of trade, if re-employed within 6 months after the day
-- stood down; (c) absence with the employer's leave, other than the leave in paragraphs (a) and (b) of the dictionary
-- definition of continuous service; (d) absence because of injury arising out of or in the course of the employment;
-- (e) any other interruption, including ending of service by the employer (other than to avoid long service leave), if
-- the employee returns within 2 months after the day service is interrupted; (f) part-time Defence Force service;
-- (g) service temporarily outside the ACT. s 2G (3): an interruption over 2 months caused by the seasonal nature of the
-- work does not break continuity. s 2G (5): the interruption under (2) or (3), except (2) (f) and (g), is not counted.
-- Dictionary, continuous service, includes: (a) annual leave and long service leave; (b) leave of up to 2 weeks in any
-- 1 year because of illness or injury; (c) a period when the employer interrupts or ends service intending to avoid
-- granting long service leave.
DECLARE `The kind of break` IS ONE OF
    `an industrial dispute (a strike or lockout)`
    `being stood down by my employer because of slackness of trade`
    `annual leave or long service leave`
    `leave because of illness or injury not arising from my work`
    `other leave my employer allowed`
    `absence because of an injury arising out of or in the course of my employment`
    `my employer interrupting or ending my service intending to avoid granting long service leave`
    `serving in the Defence Force, other than continuous full-time service`
    `working for my employer temporarily outside the ACT`
    `a break caused by the seasonal nature of the work`
    `any other break, including my resigning or my employer ending my service`

DECLARE `What the break does to my service` IS ONE OF
    `continuity kept, and the time counts as service (dictionary, continuous service, or s 2G (2) (f) or (g))`
    `continuity kept, but the time of the break is not counted as service (s 2G (2) or (3), and s 2G (5))`
    `continuity kept: up to 2 weeks of illness or injury leave in the year counts as service; the rest is leave with your employer's permission, not counted (dictionary, continuous service (b); s 2G (2) (c), (5))`
    `continuity broken: you did not return within the time s 2G allows, so no head of s 2G (2) or (3) saves the break (the text's no)`
    `not known yet: you have not returned, and the time s 2G allows for your return has not run out`

-- s 2G (2) (e): a return within 2 months after the day service is interrupted. The fall-back for every head whose own
-- condition fails (fork F3).
GIVEN `the break began on` IS A DATE, `I have returned` IS A BOOLEAN, `I returned on` IS A DATE, `today` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `What the break does to my service`
`the s 2G (2) (e) answer` `the break began on` `I have returned` `I returned on` `today` MEANS
    IF `I have returned`
    THEN IF `the day` `I returned on` `is on or before` (`the day` 2 `months after` `the break began on`)
         THEN `continuity kept, but the time of the break is not counted as service (s 2G (2) or (3), and s 2G (5))`
         ELSE `continuity broken: you did not return within the time s 2G allows, so no head of s 2G (2) or (3) saves the break (the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `the day` `today` `is after` (`the day` 2 `months after` `the break began on`)
         THEN `continuity broken: you did not return within the time s 2G allows, so no head of s 2G (2) or (3) saves the break (the text's no)`
         ELSE `not known yet: you have not returned, and the time s 2G allows for your return has not run out`

@export Does a break in my work for my employer break the continuity of my service for long service leave, and does the time of the break count as service (ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976, s 2G and dictionary, continuous service)? Leave, work injuries, stand-downs, strikes, Defence Force service and short breaks usually keep continuity, but most breaks are not counted as service.
GIVEN `the kind of break` IS A `The kind of break` @desc What the break in your work for this employer was
      `returned under the settlement terms` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For an industrial dispute only: whether you went back to work for the employer in accordance with the terms of settlement of the dispute (otherwise any value; it is not used)
      `illness leave days this year` IS A NUMBER @desc For illness or injury leave only: how many days of such leave you took in that year (otherwise any number; it is not used)
      `the break began on` IS A DATE @desc The day the break began (for a stand-down, the day you were stood down)
      `I have returned` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have gone back to work for the employer (re-employed, for a stand-down)
      `I returned on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day you went back (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `What the break does to my service`
`s 2G -- what does this break do to my service` `the kind of break` `returned under the settlement terms` `illness leave days this year` `the break began on` `I have returned` `I returned on` `today` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the kind of break`
    WHEN `an industrial dispute (a strike or lockout)` THEN
        IF `I have returned` AND `returned under the settlement terms`
        THEN `continuity kept, but the time of the break is not counted as service (s 2G (2) or (3), and s 2G (5))`
        ELSE IF NOT `I have returned`
             THEN `not known yet: you have not returned, and the time s 2G allows for your return has not run out`
             ELSE `the s 2G (2) (e) answer` `the break began on` `I have returned` `I returned on` `today`
    WHEN `being stood down by my employer because of slackness of trade` THEN
        IF `I have returned`
        THEN IF `the day` `I returned on` `is on or before` (`the day` 6 `months after` `the break began on`)
             THEN `continuity kept, but the time of the break is not counted as service (s 2G (2) or (3), and s 2G (5))`
             ELSE `continuity broken: you did not return within the time s 2G allows, so no head of s 2G (2) or (3) saves the break (the text's no)`
        ELSE IF `the day` `today` `is after` (`the day` 6 `months after` `the break began on`)
             THEN `continuity broken: you did not return within the time s 2G allows, so no head of s 2G (2) or (3) saves the break (the text's no)`
             ELSE `not known yet: you have not returned, and the time s 2G allows for your return has not run out`
    WHEN `annual leave or long service leave` THEN `continuity kept, and the time counts as service (dictionary, continuous service, or s 2G (2) (f) or (g))`
    WHEN `leave because of illness or injury not arising from my work` THEN
        IF `illness leave days this year` AT MOST 14
        THEN `continuity kept, and the time counts as service (dictionary, continuous service, or s 2G (2) (f) or (g))`
        ELSE `continuity kept: up to 2 weeks of illness or injury leave in the year counts as service; the rest is leave with your employer's permission, not counted (dictionary, continuous service (b); s 2G (2) (c), (5))`
    WHEN `other leave my employer allowed` THEN `continuity kept, but the time of the break is not counted as service (s 2G (2) or (3), and s 2G (5))`
    WHEN `absence because of an injury arising out of or in the course of my employment` THEN `continuity kept, but the time of the break is not counted as service (s 2G (2) or (3), and s 2G (5))`
    WHEN `my employer interrupting or ending my service intending to avoid granting long service leave` THEN `continuity kept, and the time counts as service (dictionary, continuous service, or s 2G (2) (f) or (g))`
    WHEN `serving in the Defence Force, other than continuous full-time service` THEN `continuity kept, and the time counts as service (dictionary, continuous service, or s 2G (2) (f) or (g))`
    WHEN `working for my employer temporarily outside the ACT` THEN `continuity kept, and the time counts as service (dictionary, continuous service, or s 2G (2) (f) or (g))`
    WHEN `a break caused by the seasonal nature of the work` THEN `continuity kept, but the time of the break is not counted as service (s 2G (2) or (3), and s 2G (5))`
    WHEN `any other break, including my resigning or my employer ending my service` THEN `the s 2G (2) (e) answer` `the break began on` `I have returned` `I returned on` `today`

§§ `ss 10, 10A, 11 and the dictionary -- Service with an earlier employer`

DECLARE `My earlier employment` IS ONE OF
    `the business was transmitted to my present employer, and I went straight from the old employer to the new one`
    `my present employer took over a services contract from my old employer, under a contract obliging it to employ the old contractor's staff where practicable, and employed me under that obligation`
    `I worked over a continuous period for two or more companies that are all associated companies (subsidiaries, holding companies or related bodies corporate)`
    `I was an apprentice or under an approved training contract with my present employer`
    `I worked for a different, unconnected employer`

DECLARE `Does my earlier service count` IS ONE OF
    `yes: your service is not interrupted by the transmission, and your service with the first employer is service with the second (s 10)`
    `yes: your service for the first contractor and the second contractor is taken to be 1 period of service for the second contractor (s 10A)`
    `yes: you are entitled as if you had been employed throughout by the company employing you when your leave accrues (s 11)`
    `yes: the apprenticeship or training contract is part of your continuous service, because you began service within 1 year after it ended (dictionary, continuous service (d))`
    `the apprenticeship or training contract is not brought in by the dictionary, because you did not begin service within 1 year after it ended; the Act says nothing more (NOTHING-kind)`
    `no: a period of service is continuous service with a particular employer; service with an unconnected employer is not part of it (the text's no, s 2G (1))`

@export Does my service with an earlier employer, or as an apprentice, count towards my long service leave with my present employer (ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976, ss 10, 10A, 11; dictionary, continuous service (d))? A sale or transfer of the business, a change of contractor with an obligation to take on staff, a move between associated companies, and an apprenticeship followed within a year by employment can all carry service across.
GIVEN `my earlier employment` IS A `My earlier employment` @desc How your earlier service came about
      `the apprenticeship ended on` IS A DATE @desc For an apprenticeship or approved training contract only: the day it ended (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `I began service on` IS A DATE @desc For an apprenticeship or approved training contract only: the day you began service as an employee of the same employer (otherwise any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A `Does my earlier service count`
`ss 10, 10A, 11 -- does my earlier service count` `my earlier employment` `the apprenticeship ended on` `I began service on` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my earlier employment`
    WHEN `the business was transmitted to my present employer, and I went straight from the old employer to the new one` THEN `yes: your service is not interrupted by the transmission, and your service with the first employer is service with the second (s 10)`
    WHEN `my present employer took over a services contract from my old employer, under a contract obliging it to employ the old contractor's staff where practicable, and employed me under that obligation` THEN `yes: your service for the first contractor and the second contractor is taken to be 1 period of service for the second contractor (s 10A)`
    WHEN `I worked over a continuous period for two or more companies that are all associated companies (subsidiaries, holding companies or related bodies corporate)` THEN `yes: you are entitled as if you had been employed throughout by the company employing you when your leave accrues (s 11)`
    WHEN `I was an apprentice or under an approved training contract with my present employer` THEN
        IF `the day` `I began service on` `is on or before` (`the day` 12 `months after` `the apprenticeship ended on`)
        THEN `yes: the apprenticeship or training contract is part of your continuous service, because you began service within 1 year after it ended (dictionary, continuous service (d))`
        ELSE `the apprenticeship or training contract is not brought in by the dictionary, because you did not begin service within 1 year after it ended; the Act says nothing more (NOTHING-kind)`
    WHEN `I worked for a different, unconnected employer` THEN `no: a period of service is continuous service with a particular employer; service with an unconnected employer is not part of it (the text's no, s 2G (1))`

§§ `ss 3, 4 -- When I become entitled, and how much leave`

-- The day a period of service of a number of whole years is completed: the anniversary of the start, moved on by the
-- interruptions that s 2G (5) does not count (whole months, then days). The Act's own example in s 2G: Fiona starts on
-- 1 January 1992; 9 months of absences are not counted; "Her 10 years long service leave falls due on 1 October 2002."
-- So the entitlement falls due on the day the years plus the uncounted time run out from the start (fork F2).
GIVEN `the years` IS A NUMBER, `I started on` IS A DATE, `months not counted` IS A NUMBER, `days not counted` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the day` `the years` `years of service are completed from` `I started on` `months not counted` `days not counted` MEANS
    `the date` `days not counted` `days after` (`the day` ((`the years` TIMES 12) PLUS `months not counted`) `months after` `I started on`)

-- Completed whole years of service on a day: the start moved on by the uncounted time, then whole years to that day.
GIVEN `I started on` IS A DATE, `months not counted` IS A NUMBER, `days not counted` IS A NUMBER, `on the day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the completed years of service` `I started on` `months not counted` `days not counted` `on the day` MEANS
    TRUNC (`the whole months from` `the counted start` `to` `on the day` DIVIDED BY 12) 0
    WHERE
        `the counted start` MEANS `the date` `days not counted` `days after` (`the day` `months not counted` `months after` `I started on`)

-- Completed whole months of service on a day (used for the s 11C proportion).
GIVEN `I started on` IS A DATE, `months not counted` IS A NUMBER, `days not counted` IS A NUMBER, `on the day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the completed months of service` `I started on` `months not counted` `days not counted` `on the day` MEANS
    `the whole months from` (`the date` `days not counted` `days after` (`the day` `months not counted` `months after` `I started on`)) `to` `on the day`

@export On what day do I become entitled to long service leave (ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976, s 3)? You become entitled once you complete 7 years of service with a single employer; breaks that s 2G does not count (most breaks other than paid leave) push the day back.
GIVEN `I started on` IS A DATE @desc The day your period of service with this employer began (including earlier service the Act carries across, such as service before a transfer of the business)
      `months not counted` IS A NUMBER @desc The whole months of breaks in your service that are not counted as service (for example unpaid leave, a strike, a stand-down, time off for a work injury); 0 if none
      `days not counted` IS A NUMBER @desc Any further days of such breaks, beyond the whole months; 0 if none
GIVETH A DATE
`s 3 -- the day I become entitled to long service leave` `I started on` `months not counted` `days not counted` MEANS
    `the day` 7 `years of service are completed from` `I started on` `months not counted` `days not counted`

@export How many whole years of service have I completed with my employer, for long service leave (ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976, ss 2G, 3)? Breaks that are not counted as service are left out.
GIVEN `I started on` IS A DATE @desc The day your period of service with this employer began (including earlier service the Act carries across)
      `months not counted` IS A NUMBER @desc The whole months of breaks in your service that are not counted as service; 0 if none
      `days not counted` IS A NUMBER @desc Any further days of such breaks, beyond the whole months; 0 if none
      `on the day` IS A DATE @desc The day to count to (today, or the day your employment ended)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 3 -- how many years of service have I completed` `I started on` `months not counted` `days not counted` `on the day` MEANS
    `the completed years of service` `I started on` `months not counted` `days not counted` `on the day`

-- s 3 (1): entitled on completing 7 years with a single employer, for the period of the service; s 3 (2): for each year
-- completed after the 7th; s 4: at the rate of 1/5 of a month's leave for each year of service. Only whole years count
-- (s 3 (2) "each year of service completed"). Fewer than 7 years: 0 (fork F4; ss 11A and 11C deal with leaving).
GIVEN `the completed years` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the months of leave for` `the completed years` MEANS
    IF `the completed years` AT LEAST 7
    THEN `the completed years` TIMES (1 DIVIDED BY 5)
    ELSE 0

@export How many months of long service leave have I become entitled to (ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976, ss 3, 4)? After 7 complete years of service you are entitled to 1/5 of a month's leave for each complete year (1.4 months at 7 years, 2 months at 10 years); before 7 years, none. Leave already taken is not subtracted here.
GIVEN `I started on` IS A DATE @desc The day your period of service with this employer began (including earlier service the Act carries across)
      `months not counted` IS A NUMBER @desc The whole months of breaks in your service that are not counted as service; 0 if none
      `days not counted` IS A NUMBER @desc Any further days of such breaks, beyond the whole months; 0 if none
      `on the day` IS A DATE @desc The day to count to (today, or the day your employment ended)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 4 -- how many months of long service leave am I entitled to` `I started on` `months not counted` `days not counted` `on the day` MEANS
    `the months of leave for` (`the completed years of service` `I started on` `months not counted` `days not counted` `on the day`)

§§ `ss 2D, 2E, 2EA -- Portable long service leave schemes`

DECLARE `The portable scheme` IS ONE OF
    `the building and construction industry scheme (Long Service Leave (Building and Construction Industry) Act 1981)`
    `the contract cleaning industry scheme (Long Service Leave (Contract Cleaning Industry) Act 1999)`
    `a scheme under the Long Service Leave (Portable Schemes) Act 2009`
    `none`

DECLARE `Does a portable scheme affect my claim` IS ONE OF
    `no effect: these sections deal only with employees registered under a portable scheme who elected to take its benefits; this Act applies to you as usual (NOTHING-kind)`
    `your election under the portable scheme does not stop you receiving benefits under this Act (ss 2D (2), 2E (2), 2EA (2))`
    `no: you are not entitled to a benefit under this Act for a period for which you received a benefit under the portable scheme (the text's no, ss 2D (3), 2E (3), 2EA (3))`

@export Does being in a portable long service leave scheme (building and construction, contract cleaning, or the Portable Schemes Act) stop me claiming under the ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976 (ss 2D, 2E, 2EA)? Electing to take the scheme's benefits does not stop you, but you cannot get a benefit under this Act for a period for which the scheme paid you.
GIVEN `the scheme` IS A `The portable scheme` @desc Which portable long service leave scheme you were registered under, if any
      `elected to take the scheme's benefits` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you elected under that scheme to take its long service benefits for a period you stated
      `received a scheme benefit for this period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have received a benefit under that scheme for the period of service you are now claiming for
GIVETH A `Does a portable scheme affect my claim`
`ss 2D, 2E, 2EA -- does a portable scheme affect my claim` `the scheme` `elected to take the scheme's benefits` `received a scheme benefit for this period` MEANS
    IF `the scheme` EQUALS `none` OR NOT `elected to take the scheme's benefits`
    THEN `no effect: these sections deal only with employees registered under a portable scheme who elected to take its benefits; this Act applies to you as usual (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF `received a scheme benefit for this period`
         THEN `no: you are not entitled to a benefit under this Act for a period for which you received a benefit under the portable scheme (the text's no, ss 2D (3), 2E (3), 2EA (3))`
         ELSE `your election under the portable scheme does not stop you receiving benefits under this Act (ss 2D (2), 2E (2), 2EA (2))`

§§ `s 6 -- Taking my leave`

-- s 6 (1): an employer commits an offence (50 penalty units, strict liability) if an employee becomes entitled to 4 weeks
-- or more of long service leave and the employer does not grant it (i) as soon as practicable, having regard to the
-- needs of the employer's business, after the employee becomes entitled, or (ii) if they agree, at another time or
-- times. Leave is counted in months (s 4) and the test in weeks. A calendar month has 28 to 31 days, so 1 month or more
-- is always 4 weeks or more, and under 28/31 of a month is always less; in between the answer turns on which month,
-- which the Act does not say (fork F5).
DECLARE `Must my employer grant my leave` IS ONE OF
    `yes, at the time or times you and your employer agreed (s 6 (1) (b) (ii)); not granting it then is an offence (50 penalty units)`
    `yes, as soon as practicable after you became entitled, having regard to the needs of your employer's business (s 6 (1) (b) (i)); not granting it is an offence (50 penalty units)`
    `s 6 (1) puts no duty on your employer: your untaken leave is less than 4 weeks, and the Act says nothing about when shorter leave must be granted (NOTHING-kind)`
    `it depends on the length of the month: your untaken leave is between 28/31 of a month and 1 month, and the Act does not say how a month of leave converts to weeks (NOTHING-kind)`
    `not yet: you have not completed 7 years of service, so you are not yet entitled to long service leave (s 3 (1))`

@export Must my employer let me take my long service leave, and when (ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976, s 6 (1))? Once you are entitled to 4 weeks or more, your employer must grant it as soon as practicable having regard to its business needs, or at a time you both agree.
GIVEN `the completed years` IS A NUMBER @desc How many whole years of service you have completed with this employer (breaks not counted as service left out)
      `months of leave already taken` IS A NUMBER @desc How many months of long service leave you have already been granted by this employer (0 if none)
      `agreed another time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and your employer have agreed on a time or times for the leave
GIVETH A `Must my employer grant my leave`
`s 6 -- must my employer grant my leave` `the completed years` `months of leave already taken` `agreed another time` MEANS
    IF `the completed years` LESS THAN 7
    THEN `not yet: you have not completed 7 years of service, so you are not yet entitled to long service leave (s 3 (1))`
    ELSE IF `the untaken months` LESS THAN (28 DIVIDED BY 31)
         THEN `s 6 (1) puts no duty on your employer: your untaken leave is less than 4 weeks, and the Act says nothing about when shorter leave must be granted (NOTHING-kind)`
         ELSE IF `the untaken months` LESS THAN 1
              THEN `it depends on the length of the month: your untaken leave is between 28/31 of a month and 1 month, and the Act does not say how a month of leave converts to weeks (NOTHING-kind)`
              ELSE IF `agreed another time`
                   THEN `yes, at the time or times you and your employer agreed (s 6 (1) (b) (ii)); not granting it then is an offence (50 penalty units)`
                   ELSE `yes, as soon as practicable after you became entitled, having regard to the needs of your employer's business (s 6 (1) (b) (i)); not granting it is an offence (50 penalty units)`
    WHERE
        `the untaken months` MEANS `the months of leave for` `the completed years` MINUS `months of leave already taken`

-- The parties and their steps.
DECLARE `LSL party` IS ONE OF `the employee`, `the employer`

DECLARE `LSL step` IS ONE OF
    `give written notice of the day the leave starts`
    `pay the employee for the leave credit`
    `comply with the authorised officer's notice`

-- s 6 (2), (4): the employer commits an offence (50 penalty units, strict liability) if it does not, at least 60 days
-- before the date from which long service leave is to be taken, give the employee written notice of the date. "At least
-- 60 days before" is read as the notice day being 60 or more days before the start day (fork F6). Time is counted in day
-- serials: the deadline is the serial of the last day for the notice.
GIVEN `the last day serial` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `LSL party` `LSL step`
`the employer must give 60 days' written notice` `the last day serial` MEANS
    PARTY `the employer`
    MUST `give written notice of the day the leave starts`
    WITHIN `the last day serial`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "no written notice of the leave's start date at least 60 days before it: an offence (s 6 (2), 50 penalty units, strict liability)"

@export By what day must my employer give me written notice of the day my long service leave starts (ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976, s 6 (2))? At least 60 days before that day, unless you agree otherwise.
GIVEN `the leave starts on` IS A DATE @desc The day your long service leave is to start
GIVETH A DATE
`s 6 (2) -- the last day for written notice of my leave` `the leave starts on` MEANS
    `the date` (0 MINUS 60) `days after` `the leave starts on`

DECLARE `My notice of leave` IS ONE OF
    `s 6 (2) does not apply: you agreed otherwise (s 6 (3))`
    `enough notice: your employer gave you written notice at least 60 days before your leave starts (s 6 (2))`
    `not enough notice: written notice came less than 60 days before your leave starts; that is an offence by your employer (the text's no, s 6 (2), 50 penalty units)`
    `no written notice yet, and there is still time for 60 days' notice`
    `no written notice, and it is now too late for 60 days' notice: that is an offence by your employer (s 6 (2), 50 penalty units)`

@export Did my employer give me enough written notice of the day my long service leave starts (ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976, s 6 (2), (3))? It must give written notice at least 60 days before, unless you agree otherwise.
GIVEN `the leave starts on` IS A DATE @desc The day your long service leave is to start
      `I agreed otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you agreed to a different arrangement for notice (for example shorter notice)
      `notice given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your employer has given you written notice of the start day
      `notice given on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day it gave the notice (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `My notice of leave`
`s 6 (2) -- did I get enough notice of my leave` `the leave starts on` `I agreed otherwise` `notice given` `notice given on` `today` MEANS
    IF `I agreed otherwise`
    THEN `s 6 (2) does not apply: you agreed otherwise (s 6 (3))`
    ELSE CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the employer must give 60 days' written notice` (DATE_SERIAL `the last day`)) 0 `the events`
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `enough notice: your employer gave you written notice at least 60 days before your leave starts (s 6 (2))`
         OTHERWISE
            IF `notice given`
            THEN `not enough notice: written notice came less than 60 days before your leave starts; that is an offence by your employer (the text's no, s 6 (2), 50 penalty units)`
            ELSE IF `the day` `today` `is after` `the last day`
                 THEN `no written notice, and it is now too late for 60 days' notice: that is an offence by your employer (s 6 (2), 50 penalty units)`
                 ELSE `no written notice yet, and there is still time for 60 days' notice`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS `s 6 (2) -- the last day for written notice of my leave` `the leave starts on`
        `the events` MEANS
            IF `notice given`
            THEN LIST (WAITUNTIL (DATE_SERIAL `notice given on`)), (EVENT `the employer` `give written notice of the day the leave starts` (DATE_SERIAL `notice given on`))
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL (DATE_SERIAL `today`))

§§ `ss 7, 8, 9 -- Pay for my leave`

-- s 7 (1): payment at the ordinary remuneration the employee would have received for the period of leave.
-- s 7 (2): for a part-time or casual employee, the average hours worked each week in the 12 months immediately before
-- the day of entitlement, multiplied by "the ordinary remuneration of the employee on that day" (read as the hourly
-- rate: finding 3). s 7 (3): if within the 2 years before the entitlement the employee ceased full-time work and became
-- part-time or casual, the total salary or wages paid in the 5 years ending on the entitlement, divided by 5. That is a
-- yearly figure; the weekly rate is taken as a 52nd of it, as s 2F counts a year (fork F7, finding 4).
DECLARE `How I am employed` IS ONE OF
    `full-time`
    `part-time or casual`
    `part-time or casual, after working full-time within the 2 years before I became entitled`

@export At what weekly rate must I be paid while on long service leave (ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976, s 7)? Full-time: your ordinary weekly remuneration. Part-time or casual: your average weekly hours over the 12 months before you became entitled, times your hourly rate. If you went from full-time to part-time or casual in the 2 years before, a fifth of your pay over the 5 years, as a weekly rate.
GIVEN `how I am employed` IS A `How I am employed` @desc Whether you are full-time, part-time or casual, and whether you moved from full-time to part-time or casual in the 2 years before you became entitled
      `my ordinary weekly remuneration` IS A NUMBER @desc For full-time: your ordinary remuneration for a week, in dollars (salary or wages without overtime or penalty rates, plus skill, qualification, board and lodging allowances and usual bonuses); otherwise any number
      `my average weekly hours over the 12 months` IS A NUMBER @desc For part-time or casual: the average number of hours you worked each week in the 12 months immediately before the day you became entitled; otherwise any number
      `my hourly ordinary remuneration on the day` IS A NUMBER @desc For part-time or casual: your ordinary remuneration for an hour on the day you became entitled, in dollars; otherwise any number
      `my salary or wages over the 5 years` IS A NUMBER @desc If you moved from full-time to part-time or casual in the 2 years before you became entitled: the total salary or wages paid to you in the 5 years ending on that day, in dollars; otherwise any number
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 7 -- my weekly rate of pay for long service leave` `how I am employed` `my ordinary weekly remuneration` `my average weekly hours over the 12 months` `my hourly ordinary remuneration on the day` `my salary or wages over the 5 years` MEANS
    CONSIDER `how I am employed`
    WHEN `full-time` THEN `my ordinary weekly remuneration`
    WHEN `part-time or casual` THEN `my average weekly hours over the 12 months` TIMES `my hourly ordinary remuneration on the day`
    WHEN `part-time or casual, after working full-time within the 2 years before I became entitled` THEN (`my salary or wages over the 5 years` DIVIDED BY 5) DIVIDED BY 52

-- s 8 (1), (2): an offence (50 penalty units, strict liability) unless the employer pays for the leave (a) in advance
-- for the whole period; or (b) at the same times as it would have paid the employee had the leave not been taken and,
-- if the employee asks, by cheque posted to an address the employee nominates; or (c) if they agree, in another way.
DECLARE `How I am being paid for my leave` IS ONE OF
    `in advance, for the whole period of the leave`
    `at my usual pay times, as if I were working`
    `in another way I agreed with my employer`
    `in another way I did not agree to`

DECLARE `Is the way I am paid for my leave allowed` IS ONE OF
    `yes: payment in advance for the whole period (s 8 (1) (a))`
    `yes: payment at your usual pay times (s 8 (1) (b))`
    `no: you asked to be paid by cheque posted to an address you nominated, and that was not done; that is an offence by your employer (the text's no, s 8 (1) (b), 50 penalty units)`
    `yes: payment in a way you and your employer agreed (s 8 (1) (c))`
    `no: payment for long service leave must be in advance, at your usual pay times, or as you agree; anything else is an offence by your employer (the text's no, s 8 (1), 50 penalty units)`

@export Is my employer paying me for long service leave in a way the Act allows (ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976, s 8 (1))? It must pay in advance for the whole leave, or at your usual pay times (by posted cheque if you ask), or in another way you agree.
GIVEN `how I am being paid` IS A `How I am being paid for my leave` @desc How your employer is paying you for the leave
      `I asked for a posted cheque` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For payment at your usual pay times only: whether you asked to be paid by cheque posted to an address you nominated
      `I got a posted cheque` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you asked, whether your employer paid you that way
GIVETH AN `Is the way I am paid for my leave allowed`
`s 8 -- is the way I am paid for my leave allowed` `how I am being paid` `I asked for a posted cheque` `I got a posted cheque` MEANS
    CONSIDER `how I am being paid`
    WHEN `in advance, for the whole period of the leave` THEN `yes: payment in advance for the whole period (s 8 (1) (a))`
    WHEN `at my usual pay times, as if I were working` THEN
        IF `I asked for a posted cheque` AND NOT `I got a posted cheque`
        THEN `no: you asked to be paid by cheque posted to an address you nominated, and that was not done; that is an offence by your employer (the text's no, s 8 (1) (b), 50 penalty units)`
        ELSE `yes: payment at your usual pay times (s 8 (1) (b))`
    WHEN `in another way I agreed with my employer` THEN `yes: payment in a way you and your employer agreed (s 8 (1) (c))`
    WHEN `in another way I did not agree to` THEN `no: payment for long service leave must be in advance, at your usual pay times, or as you agree; anything else is an offence by your employer (the text's no, s 8 (1), 50 penalty units)`

-- s 8 (3): if, after payment, the ordinary remuneration payable under an award or agreement increases, the increase is
-- paid (a) if paid in advance, as soon as practicable on completion of the leave; (b) if paid at the usual times, in the
-- same way; (c) if paid another agreed way, as agreed, or failing agreement as soon as practicable on completion.
DECLARE `When I get a pay rise that falls during my leave` IS ONE OF
    `as soon as practicable when your leave ends (s 8 (3) (a))`
    `at your usual pay times, as the rest of your leave pay (s 8 (3) (b))`
    `as you and your employer agreed (s 8 (3) (c))`
    `as soon as practicable when your leave ends, since you and your employer have not agreed how (s 8 (3) (c))`
    `s 8 (3) covers only payment made in a way s 8 (1) allows; the Act says nothing about a rise paid with leave paid some other way (NOTHING-kind)`

@export When must I be paid an award or agreement pay rise that takes effect after I was paid for my long service leave (ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976, s 8 (3))? It depends on how the leave was paid.
GIVEN `how I am being paid` IS A `How I am being paid for my leave` @desc How your employer paid you for the leave
      `we agreed how to pay the rise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For leave paid in another agreed way only: whether you and your employer agreed how a pay rise would be paid
GIVETH A `When I get a pay rise that falls during my leave`
`s 8 (3) -- when do I get a pay rise during my leave` `how I am being paid` `we agreed how to pay the rise` MEANS
    CONSIDER `how I am being paid`
    WHEN `in advance, for the whole period of the leave` THEN `as soon as practicable when your leave ends (s 8 (3) (a))`
    WHEN `at my usual pay times, as if I were working` THEN `at your usual pay times, as the rest of your leave pay (s 8 (3) (b))`
    WHEN `in another way I agreed with my employer` THEN
        IF `we agreed how to pay the rise`
        THEN `as you and your employer agreed (s 8 (3) (c))`
        ELSE `as soon as practicable when your leave ends, since you and your employer have not agreed how (s 8 (3) (c))`
    WHEN `in another way I did not agree to` THEN `s 8 (3) covers only payment made in a way s 8 (1) allows; the Act says nothing about a rise paid with leave paid some other way (NOTHING-kind)`

-- s 9: the period of long service leave is increased by 1 day for each public holiday or award holiday falling during it.
@export By how many days is my long service leave extended for public holidays (ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976, s 9)? One day for each public holiday or award holiday that falls during the leave.
GIVEN `public holidays during my leave` IS A NUMBER @desc How many public holidays fall during your long service leave
      `award holidays during my leave` IS A NUMBER @desc How many other days that are holidays under your award or agreement fall during the leave (not counting public holidays twice)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 9 -- how many days is my leave extended for holidays` `public holidays during my leave` `award holidays during my leave` MEANS
    `public holidays during my leave` PLUS `award holidays during my leave`

§§ `ss 11A, 11C, 11D -- When my employment ends (only if your employment has ended)`

-- s 11D: for ss 11A and 11C the ordinary remuneration is at the rate payable immediately before the employment ended,
-- and for a part-time or casual employee on the average hours a week in the 12 months immediately before it ended.
-- A month of leave is paid as 52/12 weeks (fork F8).
DECLARE `How I was employed at the end` IS ONE OF
    `full-time at the end`
    `part-time or casual at the end`

GIVEN `how I was employed` IS A `How I was employed at the end`, `my ordinary weekly remuneration` IS A NUMBER,
      `my average weekly hours` IS A NUMBER, `my hourly ordinary remuneration` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the s 11D weekly rate` `how I was employed` `my ordinary weekly remuneration` `my average weekly hours` `my hourly ordinary remuneration` MEANS
    CONSIDER `how I was employed`
    WHEN `full-time at the end` THEN `my ordinary weekly remuneration`
    WHEN `part-time or casual at the end` THEN `my average weekly hours` TIMES `my hourly ordinary remuneration`

GIVEN `the months` IS A NUMBER, `the weekly rate` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the pay for` `the months` `months of leave at` `the weekly rate` MEANS
    `the months` TIMES (52 DIVIDED BY 12) TIMES `the weekly rate`

-- s 11A (1)-(3): the long service leave credit is the leave accrued less the leave granted. Where employment ends for any
-- reason and the person had a credit, the employer must pay the person (or, if dead, the legal personal representative)
-- the ordinary remuneration for a period equal to the credit.
@export How much must my employer pay me for my untaken long service leave when my employment ends (ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976, ss 11A, 11D)? Whatever the reason the employment ended, you (or, if you have died, your legal personal representative) must be paid for the leave you were entitled to and had not taken, at your rate immediately before the end.
GIVEN `the completed years` IS A NUMBER @desc How many whole years of service you had completed with this employer when the employment ended (breaks not counted as service left out)
      `months of leave already taken` IS A NUMBER @desc How many months of long service leave this employer had already granted you (0 if none)
      `how I was employed` IS A `How I was employed at the end` @desc Whether you were full-time, or part-time or casual, immediately before the employment ended
      `my ordinary weekly remuneration` IS A NUMBER @desc For full-time: your ordinary remuneration for a week immediately before the employment ended, in dollars; otherwise any number
      `my average weekly hours` IS A NUMBER @desc For part-time or casual: the average hours a week you worked in the 12 months immediately before the employment ended; otherwise any number
      `my hourly ordinary remuneration` IS A NUMBER @desc For part-time or casual: your ordinary remuneration for an hour immediately before the employment ended, in dollars; otherwise any number
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 11A -- how much am I owed for untaken leave when my employment ends` `the completed years` `months of leave already taken` `how I was employed` `my ordinary weekly remuneration` `my average weekly hours` `my hourly ordinary remuneration` MEANS
    IF `the credit` GREATER THAN 0
    THEN `the pay for` `the credit` `months of leave at` (`the s 11D weekly rate` `how I was employed` `my ordinary weekly remuneration` `my average weekly hours` `my hourly ordinary remuneration`)
    ELSE 0
    WHERE
        `the credit` MEANS `the months of leave for` `the completed years` MINUS `months of leave already taken`

-- s 11A (4), (5): an offence (50 penalty units, strict liability) if the employer, required to pay under s 11A (2), does
-- not pay within 90 days after the day the employment ends. Read as: the 90th day after that day is the last day.
GIVEN `the last day serial` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `LSL party` `LSL step`
`the employer must pay the leave credit within 90 days` `the last day serial` MEANS
    PARTY `the employer`
    MUST `pay the employee for the leave credit`
    WITHIN `the last day serial`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "the leave credit was not paid within 90 days after the employment ended: an offence (s 11A (4), 50 penalty units, strict liability)"

@export By what day must my employer pay me for my untaken long service leave after my employment ends (ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976, s 11A (4))? Within 90 days after the day the employment ends.
GIVEN `my employment ended on` IS A DATE @desc The day your employment ended
GIVETH A DATE
`s 11A (4) -- the last day for payment of my leave credit` `my employment ended on` MEANS
    `the date` 90 `days after` `my employment ended on`

DECLARE `Was my leave credit paid in time` IS ONE OF
    `s 11A does not apply: you had no long service leave credit when the employment ended (the text's no, s 11A (2) (b))`
    `paid in time: within 90 days after your employment ended (s 11A (4))`
    `paid late: more than 90 days after your employment ended; that is an offence by your employer (s 11A (4), 50 penalty units), and the amount was owed all along`
    `not paid yet, and the 90 days have not run out`
    `not paid, and the 90 days have run out: that is an offence by your employer (s 11A (4), 50 penalty units); you are still owed the amount`

@export Has my employer paid me for my untaken long service leave in time after my employment ended (ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976, s 11A (2), (4))? It must pay within 90 days after the day the employment ends.
GIVEN `I had a leave credit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had long service leave you were entitled to and had not taken when the employment ended
      `my employment ended on` IS A DATE @desc The day your employment ended
      `paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your employer has paid you for the untaken leave
      `paid on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day it paid (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Was my leave credit paid in time`
`s 11A (4) -- was my leave credit paid in time` `I had a leave credit` `my employment ended on` `paid` `paid on` `today` MEANS
    IF NOT `I had a leave credit`
    THEN `s 11A does not apply: you had no long service leave credit when the employment ended (the text's no, s 11A (2) (b))`
    ELSE CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the employer must pay the leave credit within 90 days` (DATE_SERIAL `the last day`)) 0 `the events`
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `paid in time: within 90 days after your employment ended (s 11A (4))`
         OTHERWISE
            IF `paid`
            THEN `paid late: more than 90 days after your employment ended; that is an offence by your employer (s 11A (4), 50 penalty units), and the amount was owed all along`
            ELSE IF `the day` `today` `is after` `the last day`
                 THEN `not paid, and the 90 days have run out: that is an offence by your employer (s 11A (4), 50 penalty units); you are still owed the amount`
                 ELSE `not paid yet, and the 90 days have not run out`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS `s 11A (4) -- the last day for payment of my leave credit` `my employment ended on`
        `the events` MEANS
            IF `paid`
            THEN LIST (WAITUNTIL (DATE_SERIAL `paid on`)), (EVENT `the employer` `pay the employee for the leave credit` (DATE_SERIAL `paid on`))
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL (DATE_SERIAL `today`))

-- s 11C (1): where employment is terminated (i) by the person because of illness or incapacity or a domestic or other
-- pressing necessity of a nature to justify the termination; (ii) by the person on or after attaining the minimum
-- retiring age; (iii) by the person's death; (iv) by the employer for a reason other than the person's serious and
-- wilful misconduct; and the person had completed at least 5 but less than 7 years of service, the employer must pay
-- the person (or the legal personal representative). s 11C (3): minimum retiring age is the age fixed by an award or
-- agreement that applies, otherwise 65. Whether a necessity justified the termination, and whether misconduct was
-- serious and wilful, are judgments: asked as facts.
DECLARE `How my employment ended` IS ONE OF
    `I resigned because of illness or incapacity`
    `I resigned because of a domestic or other pressing necessity`
    `I resigned or retired for another reason`
    `I died (the claim is by my legal personal representative)`
    `my employer ended it`

DECLARE `Am I owed pro rata long service leave pay` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you had at least 5 but less than 7 years of service, and the employment ended in one of the ways s 11C (1) (a) lists (s 11C (1))`
    `no: s 11C needs at least 5 years of service (the text's no, s 11C (1) (b))`
    `s 11C does not apply because you had completed 7 years: you are owed payment for your untaken leave under s 11A instead`
    `no: the employment did not end in one of the ways s 11C (1) (a) lists (the text's no, s 11C (1) (a))`

GIVEN `how it ended` IS A `How my employment ended`, `the necessity justified resigning` IS A BOOLEAN, `my age when I resigned` IS A NUMBER,
      `an award or agreement fixes a minimum retiring age` IS A BOOLEAN, `that minimum retiring age` IS A NUMBER, `for serious and wilful misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `ended in a way s 11C (1) (a) lists` `how it ended` `the necessity justified resigning` `my age when I resigned` `an award or agreement fixes a minimum retiring age` `that minimum retiring age` `for serious and wilful misconduct` IF
    CONSIDER `how it ended`
    WHEN `I resigned because of illness or incapacity` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `I resigned because of a domestic or other pressing necessity` THEN `the necessity justified resigning`
    WHEN `I resigned or retired for another reason` THEN `my age when I resigned` AT LEAST `the minimum retiring age`
    WHEN `I died (the claim is by my legal personal representative)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `my employer ended it` THEN NOT `for serious and wilful misconduct`
    WHERE
        `the minimum retiring age` MEANS
            IF `an award or agreement fixes a minimum retiring age`
            THEN `that minimum retiring age`
            ELSE 65

@export Am I owed pro rata long service leave pay because my employment ended after 5 but before 7 years (ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976, s 11C)? Only if you resigned because of illness, incapacity or a pressing necessity, retired at or after the minimum retiring age (65 unless your award or agreement fixes one), died, or were dismissed for something other than serious and wilful misconduct.
GIVEN `the completed years` IS A NUMBER @desc How many whole years of service you had completed with this employer when the employment ended (breaks not counted as service left out)
      `how it ended` IS A `How my employment ended` @desc How the employment ended
      `the necessity justified resigning` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a resignation because of a domestic or other pressing necessity: whether the necessity was of a nature that justified resigning (otherwise any value)
      `my age when I resigned` IS A NUMBER @desc For a resignation or retirement for another reason: your age in whole years when you resigned (otherwise any number)
      `an award or agreement fixes a minimum retiring age` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a minimum retiring age applies to you under a modern award, workplace determination or enterprise agreement
      `that minimum retiring age` IS A NUMBER @desc If so, that age (otherwise any number)
      `for serious and wilful misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your employer ended the employment: whether it was for your serious and wilful misconduct (otherwise any value)
GIVETH AN `Am I owed pro rata long service leave pay`
`s 11C -- am I owed pro rata long service leave pay` `the completed years` `how it ended` `the necessity justified resigning` `my age when I resigned` `an award or agreement fixes a minimum retiring age` `that minimum retiring age` `for serious and wilful misconduct` MEANS
    IF `the completed years` AT LEAST 7
    THEN `s 11C does not apply because you had completed 7 years: you are owed payment for your untaken leave under s 11A instead`
    ELSE IF `the completed years` LESS THAN 5
         THEN `no: s 11C needs at least 5 years of service (the text's no, s 11C (1) (b))`
         ELSE IF `ended in a way s 11C (1) (a) lists` `how it ended` `the necessity justified resigning` `my age when I resigned` `an award or agreement fixes a minimum retiring age` `that minimum retiring age` `for serious and wilful misconduct`
              THEN `yes: you had at least 5 but less than 7 years of service, and the employment ended in one of the ways s 11C (1) (a) lists (s 11C (1))`
              ELSE `no: the employment did not end in one of the ways s 11C (1) (a) lists (the text's no, s 11C (1) (a))`

-- s 11C (2): the amount is the ordinary remuneration for a period that bears to 2 months the same proportion as "the
-- number of years or months of service completed" bears to 10 years. Counted in completed months (fork F9): the leave is
-- completed months / 120 x 2 months. Paid at the s 11D rate. 0 where s 11C (1) does not give a payment.
@export How much pro rata long service leave pay am I owed when my employment ends after 5 but before 7 years (ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976, ss 11C (2), 11D)? 2 months' pay for every 10 years of service, in proportion to the months of service you completed, at your rate immediately before the end; 0 if s 11C gives you nothing.
GIVEN `I started on` IS A DATE @desc The day your period of service with this employer began (including earlier service the Act carries across)
      `months not counted` IS A NUMBER @desc The whole months of breaks in your service that are not counted as service; 0 if none
      `days not counted` IS A NUMBER @desc Any further days of such breaks, beyond the whole months; 0 if none
      `my employment ended on` IS A DATE @desc The day your employment ended
      `how it ended` IS A `How my employment ended` @desc How the employment ended
      `the necessity justified resigning` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a resignation because of a domestic or other pressing necessity: whether the necessity was of a nature that justified resigning (otherwise any value)
      `my age when I resigned` IS A NUMBER @desc For a resignation or retirement for another reason: your age in whole years when you resigned (otherwise any number)
      `an award or agreement fixes a minimum retiring age` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a minimum retiring age applies to you under a modern award, workplace determination or enterprise agreement
      `that minimum retiring age` IS A NUMBER @desc If so, that age (otherwise any number)
      `for serious and wilful misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your employer ended the employment: whether it was for your serious and wilful misconduct (otherwise any value)
      `how I was employed` IS A `How I was employed at the end` @desc Whether you were full-time, or part-time or casual, immediately before the employment ended
      `my ordinary weekly remuneration` IS A NUMBER @desc For full-time: your ordinary remuneration for a week immediately before the employment ended, in dollars; otherwise any number
      `my average weekly hours` IS A NUMBER @desc For part-time or casual: the average hours a week you worked in the 12 months immediately before the employment ended; otherwise any number
      `my hourly ordinary remuneration` IS A NUMBER @desc For part-time or casual: your ordinary remuneration for an hour immediately before the employment ended, in dollars; otherwise any number
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 11C -- how much pro rata long service leave pay am I owed` `I started on` `months not counted` `days not counted` `my employment ended on` `how it ended` `the necessity justified resigning` `my age when I resigned` `an award or agreement fixes a minimum retiring age` `that minimum retiring age` `for serious and wilful misconduct` `how I was employed` `my ordinary weekly remuneration` `my average weekly hours` `my hourly ordinary remuneration` MEANS
    IF `the completed months` AT LEAST 60
        AND `the completed months` LESS THAN 84
        AND `ended in a way s 11C (1) (a) lists` `how it ended` `the necessity justified resigning` `my age when I resigned` `an award or agreement fixes a minimum retiring age` `that minimum retiring age` `for serious and wilful misconduct`
    THEN `the pay for` ((`the completed months` DIVIDED BY 120) TIMES 2) `months of leave at` (`the s 11D weekly rate` `how I was employed` `my ordinary weekly remuneration` `my average weekly hours` `my hourly ordinary remuneration`)
    ELSE 0
    WHERE
        `the completed months` MEANS `the completed months of service` `I started on` `months not counted` `days not counted` `my employment ended on`

§§ `s 12 (2) -- My long service leave records (only if your service has ended)`

-- s 12 (2): the employer must keep the s 12 (1) record (a) if service ends on the employee's death, for 7 years after
-- the day all amounts owing to the legal personal representative are paid; (b) otherwise for 7 years after the day the
-- service ends. 20 penalty units. The day returned is the last day of the 7 years (fork F10).
@export Until what day must my employer keep its long service leave records about me after my service ends (ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976, s 12 (2))? For 7 years after the day the service ends, or, if it ended on your death, 7 years after everything owed to your legal personal representative is paid.
GIVEN `ended on my death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the service ended on your death (the question is then asked by your legal personal representative)
      `my service ended on` IS A DATE @desc The day the service ended
      `all amounts paid to my legal personal representative on` IS A DATE @desc If the service ended on your death: the day all amounts owing to your legal personal representative were paid (otherwise any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A DATE
`s 12 (2) -- until when must my employer keep my records` `ended on my death` `my service ended on` `all amounts paid to my legal personal representative on` MEANS
    IF `ended on my death`
    THEN `the day` 84 `months after` `all amounts paid to my legal personal representative on`
    ELSE `the day` 84 `months after` `my service ended on`

§§ `ss 13D, 13E -- If I complain (only if you have complained about your entitlements)`

-- s 13D (4) (a): "Except with the consent of the authorised officer ... an individual is not entitled to be represented
-- at a conference by another person."
DECLARE `Can someone represent me at the conference` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the authorised officer has consented (s 13D (4))`
    `no: an individual is not entitled to be represented at the conference by another person without the authorised officer's consent (the text's no, s 13D (4) (a))`

@export Can someone (a lawyer, union official or friend) represent me at a conference the authorised officer calls about my long service leave complaint (ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976, s 13D (4))? Only with the authorised officer's consent.
GIVEN `the officer consented` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the authorised officer has consented to your being represented by another person
GIVETH A `Can someone represent me at the conference`
`s 13D -- can someone represent me at the conference` `the officer consented` MEANS
    IF `the officer consented`
    THEN `yes: the authorised officer has consented (s 13D (4))`
    ELSE `no: an individual is not entitled to be represented at the conference by another person without the authorised officer's consent (the text's no, s 13D (4) (a))`

-- s 13E (1)-(3): an authorised officer may by written notice require an employer to comply with the Act within 28 days
-- after the day the employer receives the notice; not complying is an offence (50 penalty units, strict liability).
-- s 13F, 13G: the employer may seek internal review by the registrar and then ACAT review; the Act does not say whether
-- an application for review stops the 28 days running (finding 7), so review is not modelled.
GIVEN `the last day serial` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `LSL party` `LSL step`
`the employer must comply with the notice within 28 days` `the last day serial` MEANS
    PARTY `the employer`
    MUST `comply with the authorised officer's notice`
    WITHIN `the last day serial`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "the notice to comply was not complied with within 28 days after the employer received it: an offence (s 13E (2), 50 penalty units, strict liability)"

DECLARE `Did my employer comply with the notice in time` IS ONE OF
    `complied in time: within 28 days after it received the notice (s 13E (1))`
    `complied late: more than 28 days after it received the notice; that is an offence (s 13E (2), 50 penalty units)`
    `not complied yet, and the 28 days have not run out`
    `not complied, and the 28 days have run out: that is an offence by your employer (s 13E (2), 50 penalty units)`

@export After an authorised officer has given my employer a notice to comply with the ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976, has my employer complied in time (s 13E)? It must comply within 28 days after the day it received the notice.
GIVEN `my employer received the notice on` IS A DATE @desc The day your employer received the authorised officer's written notice to comply
      `complied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your employer has done what the notice required
      `complied on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day it did (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Did my employer comply with the notice in time`
`s 13E -- did my employer comply with the notice in time` `my employer received the notice on` `complied` `complied on` `today` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the employer must comply with the notice within 28 days` (DATE_SERIAL `the last day`)) 0 `the events`
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN `complied in time: within 28 days after it received the notice (s 13E (1))`
    OTHERWISE
        IF `complied`
        THEN `complied late: more than 28 days after it received the notice; that is an offence (s 13E (2), 50 penalty units)`
        ELSE IF `the day` `today` `is after` `the last day`
             THEN `not complied, and the 28 days have run out: that is an offence by your employer (s 13E (2), 50 penalty units)`
             ELSE `not complied yet, and the 28 days have not run out`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS `the date` 28 `days after` `my employer received the notice on`
        `the events` MEANS
            IF `complied`
            THEN LIST (WAITUNTIL (DATE_SERIAL `complied on`)), (EVENT `the employer` `comply with the authorised officer's notice` (DATE_SERIAL `complied on`))
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL (DATE_SERIAL `today`))

§§ `s 14 -- My award or agreement`

-- s 14 (1): subject to (2), the Act has effect despite any award or agreement to the contrary. (2): nothing in the Act
-- affects an award or agreement to the extent it gives the employee more advantageous rights. Whether a right is more
-- advantageous is a judgment: asked as a fact.
DECLARE `Does my award or agreement change my long service leave` IS ONE OF
    `the award or agreement's more advantageous rights stand (s 14 (2))`
    `no: the Act has effect despite an award or agreement to the contrary (the text's no, s 14 (1))`
    `there is no award or agreement on the point: the Act applies (s 14 (1))`

@export Does my modern award or enterprise agreement change my long service leave under the ACT Long Service Leave Act 1976 (s 14)? It cannot take away the Act's rights, but rights in it that are more advantageous to you stand.
GIVEN `an award or agreement deals with it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a modern award, workplace determination or enterprise agreement that covers you deals with long service leave differently from the Act
      `more advantageous to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so, whether its rights are more advantageous to you than the Act's
GIVETH A `Does my award or agreement change my long service leave`
`s 14 -- does my award or agreement change my long service leave` `an award or agreement deals with it` `more advantageous to me` MEANS
    IF NOT `an award or agreement deals with it`
    THEN `there is no award or agreement on the point: the Act applies (s 14 (1))`
    ELSE IF `more advantageous to me`
         THEN `the award or agreement's more advantageous rights stand (s 14 (2))`
         ELSE `no: the Act has effect despite an award or agreement to the contrary (the text's no, s 14 (1))`
